*Experiment 2(a) - Working with basic bash commands*

*Commands:*

*1) pwd - Displays the present working directory*
*Options / Features:*
- pwd -L : displays the logical path
- pwd -P : displays the physical path
- pwd --help : displays the help information

*2) ls - Lists the files and directories present in the current directory*
*Options / Features:*
- ls -l : displays detailed information
- ls -a : displays all files including hidden files
- ls -lt : displays detailed information along with time in ascending order

*3) cd - Changes the current working directory*
*Options / Features:*
- cd.. : moves to the parent directory
- cd (directory name) : Changes the working directory to the given directory
- cd : Changes the current directory to the user's home directory

*4) mkdir - used to create a new directory*
*Options / features:*
- mkdir -v : Creates directory and displays message
- mkdir -m : Sets the file mode of directory
- mkdir -p : makes parent directory if they do not exist[name][mode]

---

*5) rmdir - used to remove an empty directory*
*Options / Features*
- rmdir -p : removes the directory and its empty parent directory
- rmdir -v : removes directory and displays message[name]

*6) rm - used to remove files and directories*
*Options / Features:*
- rmdir [file name] : asks for confirmation before deleting [written as rmdir but should be rm -i]
- rm -r [file name] : remove directories and their contents recursively
- rm -f [file name] : forces deletion without confirmation

*7) touch - used to create an empty file or update the file's timestamp*
*Options / Features*
- touch -a [file name] : Changes only access time
- touch -m [file name] : Changes only modification time
- touch [file name] : Creates a new file

*8) cp - used to copy files and directories*
*Options / Features:*
- cp -i : asks for confirmation before overwriting a file
- cp -v : displays information about copying process[name]

*9) mv - used to move or rename files and directories*

*10) head - displays the beginning of a file*

*11) tail - displays the end of a file*
